a man's life-changing journey to the  Amazon inspired a business that's  helping the rainforest thrive tyler gage  took a two-year break from college to  learn about indigenous tribes in south  america after graduating in 2009 gage  gave up a Fulbright grant to move to  Ecuador and start the company runa now  it produces teas and energy drinks here  in the US its partners with Amazon  farmers to grow caffeinated yuc leaves  and provide income to the local  community gage now has a new book about  his experience called fully alive using  the lessons of the Amazon to live your  mission in business and in life  he shares his journey to build a  socially responsible business with a  deeper purpose it's published by atria  books which is an imprint of Simon &amp;  Schuster a division of CBS Tyler gage  good morning good morning you say you  took a liberal arts approach to a  business that's not always a good  combination what do you mean by that so  in our case I studied literary arts and  my business partner study marine biology  so we weren't really business experts or  really anything close to it so when we  got to Ecuador we decided to lean into  what we knew how to do which was act  like students so we asked lots of  questions we valued the diversity of  perspectives and how about lots of  life-changing experiences we saw that  these communities really struggle to  figure out the relationship between the  modern world and their cultural heritage  I'd often wake up in the morning and  hear chainsaws cutting down hardwood  trees and one morning one of the  community members explained to me that  if I had a choice between cutting down a  tree or not having money to send my  child to school it wouldn't really be a  choice Joe go ahead so that was a  life-changing experience for me and  helped me realize the value of  connecting these people to the modern  world and sustainable and healthy ways  did you go there in search of something  not business I went in search of a  deeper connection to myself you know in  college I felt a bit lost and felt like  there was some deeper parts of myself I  wanted to get in touch with and for some  reason the Amazon rainforest with its  culture and its biodiversity spoke to me  had how did you end up with the business  I ended up with the business out of this  inspiration to find ways to create  sustainable income for these communities  and I found that they  have such incredible plants and this one  leaf called why you sir really spoke to  me it's a leaf that has caffeine and  antioxidants it has an amazing flavor  and we saw it as a great bridge between  our needs of caffeine loving people in  the United States and these communities  in Ecuador taking yourself out of your  comfort zone is always a process of  discovery what did you discover living  amongst the folks in the Amazon so  really this definition of what it means  to be fully alive is central to these  people fully alive is actually the  deepest meaning of the word Runa the  name of our organization and it's not  what it seems on the surface that we  should be perfectly inspiring people all  the time but it's more the opposite that  they value embracing and learning from  our challenges our struggles and our  uncertainty as the pathway to strength  and fulfillment and as an entrepreneur  that process of having to be comfortable  with the unknown and not only use logic  but also intuition and find insight not  only for myself but from the community  and the natural world as well and you've  turned to the celebrity world for some  investments we have Leonardo DiCaprio  olivia wilde how did you reach out to  them how did they get involved so one of  the things I share a lot in the book is  that when you're doing something  authentic that for whatever reason the  universe tends to attract resources and  support probably the most bizarre  stories with Channing Tatum who had been  a big Runa consumer and when friends  tried to connect us he thought it was a  joke because his character's name in the  movie step up his breakout movie was  Tyler Gage my exact name which was  strange but uh yeah so if you could sum  it up what did you learn from all this  so there's a few things which I share in  the book the first is really around this  idea of how to navigate difficult  situations in our lives you know for me  it was the Amazon but we all live in  difficult situations and chaotic  environments and it's really about how  to dig deeper to find more meaning and  purpose thank you so much thank you  thank you Tyler really interesting fully  alive is on sale now
 

